  "worked_solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the right of ${a}_{21}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -5 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-5{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+3{C}_{1}.$",
